<Layout
  title="Disclaimer — Earthsong"
  description="What Earthsong is and isn't: metaphor versus science, cultural sourcing, symbols, astronomical cycles, and eclipse eye safety, gathered in one place."
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Policy · Last reviewed August 8, 2026"
    title="What Earthsong is, and isn't"
    description="Earthsong tells stories, borrows images, and reflects on the sky. This page gathers the specific caveats that belong to that work, so they live in one place instead of scattered through the site."
  />

  <article class="policy-prose prose prose-lg mt-12 max-w-3xl prose-earthsong">
    <aside class="not-prose rounded-lg border border-moss/20 bg-white/60 p-6 shadow-field sm:p-8" aria-labelledby="disclaimer-short-version">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-ember-dark">The short version</p>
      <h2 id="disclaimer-short-version" class="policy-card-title mt-3 font-display text-4xl uppercase tracking-[0.05em] text-ink">Metaphor, not authority.</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 font-body text-sm leading-[1.8] text-stone">
        Earthsong is a thought experiment, not a religion, a science, or a claim on anyone else's tradition. Its stories are metaphors, its symbols are inventions, and its account of the sky is astronomy first. Where any of that touches evidence, culture, or physical safety, the specifics are below.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <h2 id="philosophy-not-religion">Not a religion, system, or authority</h2>

    <p>
      Earthsong is one evolving answer to a practical question, not a religion, a finished system, or a claim to authority. It is an invitation to explore and reflect, not a request for membership or belief.
    </p>

    <p>
      Harmony, the site's central idea, is a working hypothesis, not a program, pledge, faith, or membership test. No one needs Earthsong's language to care well for a place, and symbolic action should not be confused with measurable improvement. The idea earns its keep only when it helps someone learn more accurately, choose more carefully, or repair something that matters — and it should change when better evidence or experience appears.
    </p>

    <h2 id="metaphor-not-science">The Origin Symphony is metaphor, not science</h2>

    <p>
      The Codex's opening chapter tells a creation story about belonging. It is not a scientific account of how the universe began, and it does not replace — or quietly fill the gaps in — the evidence-based explanations of cosmology, geology, and evolutionary biology it references. Those explanations rest on evidence and remain open to refinement; the story does not.
    </p>

    <p>
      Related phrases such as <em>the living Earth</em> or <em>the world sings</em> are relational language pointing at ecological interdependence. They do not claim that a rock is biologically alive, that nature has one intention, or that a hidden vibration explains physical events.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cultural-sourcing">Learning from other traditions, without borrowing them</h2>

    <p>
      The Codex's second chapter draws on ecology, astronomy, poetry, and conservation. Acknowledging that influence is not permission to claim another community's identity, ceremony, symbol, or authority.
    </p>

    <p>
      Earthsong does not present other peoples' traditions as its own ancient roots. It has no inherited right to combine them, conduct their ceremonies, rename their sacred places, or recruit their ancestors and spiritual beings into its own story. There is no single "Indigenous spirituality," just as there is no single Native American, Aboriginal Australian, Māori, Andean, African, Asian, or European worldview — and turning them into a list of universal nature teachings would erase the very relationships this site hopes to respect.
    </p>

    <h2 id="symbols-not-truth">Symbols are inventions, not universal truths</h2>

    <p>
      The Codex's third chapter uses stone, water, flame, and other images as ways of thinking. These are creative interpretations that belong to Earthsong. They are not evidence that nature assigned one fixed meaning to an object, animal, or event, not universal spiritual truths, and not borrowed cultural identities.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Moon's phases, specifically, are offered as prompts for attention, not as forces that determine human behavior. The four poetic elements (earth, water, fire, air) are categories for reflection, not a scientific description of matter, and not a claim that all cultures interpret them alike.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cycles-not-fate">Cycles are observable, not fate</h2>

    <p>
      The Codex's sixth chapter describes solstices, equinoxes, moon phases, and eclipses as astronomy. No astronomical event dictates a decision, personality, mood, or future. A cycle can offer a reliable time to pause without becoming a force that controls human life — any personal meaning belongs to the observer, not the sky.
    </p>

    <h2 id="eclipse-safety">Eclipse eye safety</h2>

    <p>
      Never look directly at the Sun — including during the partial phases of a solar eclipse — without certified solar filters (eclipse glasses or filters meeting the ISO 12312-2 standard). Ordinary sunglasses are not sufficient, no matter how dark.
    </p>

    <p>
      Direct, unfiltered viewing is safe only during the brief total phase of a total solar eclipse, when the Moon completely covers the Sun's disk, and only from within the path of totality. Outside that narrow window — and at every point during a partial or annular eclipse — solar filters are required at all times. Earthsong is not a source of medical or safety certification; see <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/moon/eclipses/">NASA's guidance on eclipses</a> before observing one.
    </p>

